import{bt as e,n as t,ot as n,st as r}from"./chunks/framework.CxhjsvYc.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Absolute R / Absolute R+ Search Research","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://aaron-fredrick.github.io/FD-Calculator/FD-Calculator/dev/docs/development/absolute-r-plus-search-research.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Absolute R / Absolute R+ Search Research | FD Cal Documentation"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"FD Cal documentation for fixed-deposit planning, investment strategies, bank product data and the shared simulation engine."}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://aaron-fredrick.github.io/FD-Calculator/FD-Calculator/dev/docs/development/absolute-r-plus-search-research.html"}],["meta",{"name":"twitter:title","content":"Absolute R / Absolute R+ Search Research | FD Cal Documentation"}],["meta",{"name":"twitter:description","content":"FD Cal documentation for fixed-deposit planning, investment strategies, bank product data and the shared simulation engine."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/absolute-r-plus-search-research.md","filePath":"development/absolute-r-plus-search-research.md","lastUpdated":1794083319000}`),a={name:`development/absolute-r-plus-search-research.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[r(`<h1 id="absolute-r-absolute-r-search-research" tabindex="-1">Absolute R / Absolute R+ Search Research <a class="header-anchor" href="#absolute-r-absolute-r-search-research" aria-label="Permalink to “Absolute R / Absolute R+ Search Research”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Research baseline for the Absolute R+ optimisation work on branch <code>perf/absolute-r-plus-transposition-reuse</code>.</p><h2 id="objective" tabindex="-1">Objective <a class="header-anchor" href="#objective" aria-label="Permalink to “Objective”">​</a></h2><p>Improve the Scoped Range Absolute R / Absolute R+ search used by the web tool without changing financial semantics, Goal-mode semantics, Greedy fallback guarantees, or authoritative runtime replay.</p><p>The primary hypothesis is that the existing optimisation work removes repeated scalar calculations but does not eliminate enough repeated <strong>search expansion work</strong>. Absolute R+ is the production web strategy, so improvements must remain browser-compatible and bounded.</p><h2 id="current-evidence" tabindex="-1">Current evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#current-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to “Current evidence”">​</a></h2><p>The latest main-branch documentation benchmark run generated the expected horizon sweep through 60 months. Absolute R+ runtime grows materially with horizon:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Horizon</th><th style="text-align:right;">Absolute R+ runtime</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">12 months</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.615 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">24 months</td><td style="text-align:right;">6.138 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">36 months</td><td style="text-align:right;">15.197 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">48 months</td><td style="text-align:right;">24.831 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">60 months</td><td style="text-align:right;">26.802 s</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The separate Absolute R+ parameter sensitivity run at a 24-month scenario reported:</p><ul><li>beam width 32: 385 expanded states, 1.326 s</li><li>beam width 96: 2,791 expanded states, 6.151 s</li><li>beam width 256: 5,000 expanded states, 14.687 s</li><li>local branch limit 16: 2,824 expanded states, 5.637 s</li><li>local branch limit 128: 2,845 expanded states, 5.938 s</li><li>decision cap 500: 500 expanded states, 1.502 s, net interest LKR 63,302.37</li><li>decision cap 1,000: 1,000 expanded states, 3.063 s, net interest LKR 61,886.03</li><li>decision cap 5,000: 2,791 expanded states, 5.833 s, net interest LKR 61,886.03</li></ul><p>All reported Absolute R+ sensitivity points had <code>optimality_proven=false</code>.</p><p>These measurements show that search expansion and beam/local candidate generation are material costs. Increasing search budgets is not itself an optimisation and can increase work without improving the selected result.</p><h2 id="production-default-setting-finding" tabindex="-1">Production-default setting finding <a class="header-anchor" href="#production-default-setting-finding" aria-label="Permalink to “Production-default setting finding”">​</a></h2><p>The browser currently exposes Absolute R+ defaults of beam width 256 and local-branch limit 128, while the scoped benchmark uses 96 and 48 with a 5,000 decision cap. The 24-month parameter sensitivity showed the wider 256/128 configuration reaching the same reported net interest as 96/48 in that scenario while taking materially longer.</p><p>This does <strong>not</strong> justify changing production defaults yet. It is a separate configuration-quality question: the default must be chosen from broader adversarial and horizon evidence, not from one benchmark point. The implementation therefore leaves the UI defaults unchanged until the benchmark and scenario matrix establish a quality/runtime trade-off.</p><h2 id="existing-reuse-mechanisms" tabindex="-1">Existing reuse mechanisms <a class="header-anchor" href="#existing-reuse-mechanisms" aria-label="Permalink to “Existing reuse mechanisms”">​</a></h2><p>The current shared search implementation already has:</p><ul><li>bounded LRU caches for eligible-option lookup, option interest, upper bounds and priority;</li><li>a canonical Scoped state key containing date, balances, pending cash-flow schedule, obligations and paid amount;</li><li>a 50,000-entry per-run state memo/transposition table;</li><li>same-beam Absolute R+ candidate deduplication by canonical state;</li><li>authoritative replay before selecting an Absolute result;</li><li>Greedy as the financial baseline and fallback;</li><li>budget-frontier continuation through a limited number of Absolute prefixes followed by Greedy.</li></ul><p>These are useful, but they do not cache the complete expansion result of a state. The expensive work inside <code>expandState</code> includes local-combination enumeration/scoring, consolidation branch construction, cash-flow generation and child-state construction.</p><h2 id="important-distinction-state-identity-vs-expansion-reuse" tabindex="-1">Important distinction: state identity vs expansion reuse <a class="header-anchor" href="#important-distinction-state-identity-vs-expansion-reuse" aria-label="Permalink to “Important distinction: state identity vs expansion reuse”">​</a></h2><p>A canonical state match means two search paths have the same future-relevant financial state. Their historical path must remain separate for replay, but the deterministic expansion from that state can be reused.</p><p>The current memo can prune a later state when its recorded bound and operation count dominate the new arrival. That avoids a second expansion in many cases. However, it cannot reuse an already-generated child list when a state must be revisited because the new path has a lower operation count or otherwise does not satisfy the memo dominance condition.</p><p>Therefore the next optimisation should be an <strong>expansion-result transposition cache</strong>, separate from the dominance memo.</p><h2 id="safety-requirements-for-expansion-caching" tabindex="-1">Safety requirements for expansion caching <a class="header-anchor" href="#safety-requirements-for-expansion-caching" aria-label="Permalink to “Safety requirements for expansion caching”">​</a></h2><p>A cached expansion is valid only when:</p><ol><li>the canonical Scoped state is identical;</li><li>the search context and strategy settings affecting transitions are identical;</li><li>the expansion mode is identical;</li><li>the result is complete, not truncated by a decision budget;</li><li>the cached children are treated as immutable search descriptions;</li><li>child node/path metadata is reconstructed for the current parent rather than sharing mutable parent links;</li><li>Goal mode is excluded unless its provenance/path semantics are explicitly included in the key.</li></ol><p>The current implementation already represents booking-generated future events in the state&#39;s pending flow schedule. Goal mode additionally carries provenance and path-sensitive identity, so this work remains Scoped-only.</p><h2 id="beam-search-implication" tabindex="-1">Beam-search implication <a class="header-anchor" href="#beam-search-implication" aria-label="Permalink to “Beam-search implication”">​</a></h2><p>The 24-month sensitivity result where a 500-decision run found more net interest than a 1,000-decision run is not, by itself, evidence of a financial bug. Standard beam search is not generally monotonic as its search budget or beam width changes: a wider or deeper bounded beam can discard a different set of states and therefore return a different heuristic solution. This means the Absolute R+ contract must keep Greedy as the financial floor and must not assume that more search budget automatically means a better returned portfolio.</p><p>For this implementation, exact duplicate-state elimination is safer than generic beam duplicate elimination because the Scoped state key represents the complete future-relevant financial state. Historical path information is retained separately for replay and operation-count tie-breaking.</p><h2 id="decision-budget-quality-regression-root-cause-and-mitigation" tabindex="-1">Decision-budget quality regression: root cause and mitigation <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision-budget-quality-regression-root-cause-and-mitigation" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision-budget quality regression: root cause and mitigation”">​</a></h2><p>The lower-budget result can be better because budget exhaustion introduces a second candidate class: the top four states on the current beam frontier are completed by Greedy during authoritative replay. A 500-decision run evaluates its own cutoff frontier. A 1,000-decision run continues expanding that frontier, so those exact 500-decision prefixes may disappear from the final frontier and never receive the same Greedy completion. The best terminal search incumbent is retained, but the budget-frontier completion incumbent was not retained across deeper search.</p><p>A second edge case was found while making the checkpoint candidate sets comparable: the decision counter was incremented before <code>expandState</code>, while <code>expandState</code> itself used that counter to stop generating branches. Therefore the last permitted state could be only partially expanded at the exact budget boundary, while the same state was fully expanded in a higher-budget run. The counter now increments after the state has completed expansion. A cap of N therefore permits N complete state expansions and stops before expansion N+1. A focused test asserts that a one-state budget still produces a non-empty, valid frontier.</p><p>This is an implementation-level anytime-quality gap, not a claim that beam search itself is monotonic. The search order is shared up to the smaller budget; the non-monotonicity enters when each budget independently chooses a different final frontier for Greedy completion.</p><p>Mitigation implemented on this branch:</p><ul><li>Capture bounded top-four budget-frontier checkpoints every 500 expanded states.</li><li>Keep those checkpoint prefixes alongside the final cutoff frontier.</li><li>Replay checkpoint prefixes with the same authoritative Greedy continuation used for the final frontier.</li><li>Select the best valid replay across all checkpoints, terminal search incumbent and Greedy baseline.</li><li>Keep checkpointing Scoped/Absolute R+ only; Goal mode remains unchanged.</li><li>Expose the checkpoint count in search diagnostics.</li></ul><p>This makes the tested 500/1,000/5,000 decision-budget points cumulative at 500-state checkpoints: a larger budget still evaluates the earlier checkpoint portfolios rather than discarding them. It does not assert mathematical monotonicity for arbitrary non-checkpoint budgets, and the benchmark must verify that the additional bounded replay work is acceptable.</p><h2 id="follow-up-verification" tabindex="-1">Follow-up verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-up-verification" aria-label="Permalink to “Follow-up verification”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Add a deterministic multi-account, multi-product test that runs the same input at 500 and 1,000 decisions.</li><li>Assert both runs reach their configured caps, checkpoint counts are cumulative, and the higher-budget result is no worse.</li><li>Re-run the production sensitivity benchmark and inspect both quality and runtime, including the additional checkpoint replays.</li></ul><h2 id="why-browser-storage-is-not-the-first-optimisation" tabindex="-1">Why browser storage is not the first optimisation <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-browser-storage-is-not-the-first-optimisation" aria-label="Permalink to “Why browser storage is not the first optimisation”">​</a></h2><p>Persistent browser storage can reuse completed simulations between separate user runs, but it cannot safely replace intra-search transposition reuse. IndexedDB is asynchronous and introduces serialisation/versioning/invalidation complexity into a hot search path.</p><p>The first implementation should therefore use a bounded in-memory expansion cache. A later persistent result cache may be added at the simulation-result boundary, keyed by a complete input/algorithm fingerprint, if real usage demonstrates repeated identical web simulations.</p><p><code>localStorage</code> should not be used for large search-node payloads.</p><h2 id="investigation-plan" tabindex="-1">Investigation plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#investigation-plan" aria-label="Permalink to “Investigation plan”">​</a></h2><ol><li>Instrument expansion work by state and local-combination generation so the cost distribution is observable.</li><li>Add focused tests proving identical Scoped states can reuse an expansion without changing child semantics.</li><li>Memoize canonical state-key serialization without introducing strong cross-run references.</li><li>Implement a bounded per-run expansion-result cache.</li><li>Preserve the existing dominance memo as a separate pruning mechanism.</li><li>Re-run parity, search-strategy and adversarial state-identity tests.</li><li>Run the production CI benchmark and inspect Absolute R+ runtime/search diagnostics.</li><li>If expansion reuse is insufficient, investigate local-combination generation as the next hotspot rather than increasing beam width.</li><li>Only then consider an optional persistent completed-result cache for the browser.</li></ol><h2 id="explicit-non-goals" tabindex="-1">Explicit non-goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#explicit-non-goals" aria-label="Permalink to “Explicit non-goals”">​</a></h2><ul><li>No SQLite in shared browser code.</li><li>No Goal-mode state merging.</li><li>No weakening of strict outflow, provenance, maturity, fee or eFD constraints.</li><li>No assumption that a larger beam or decision budget is better.</li><li>No claim of optimality from budget exhaustion.</li><li>No new strategic preferences unsupported by authoritative replay.</li></ul><h2 id="acceptance-criteria" tabindex="-1">Acceptance criteria <a class="header-anchor" href="#acceptance-criteria" aria-label="Permalink to “Acceptance criteria”">​</a></h2><p>The optimisation is complete only if:</p><ul><li>focused and full tests pass;</li><li>canonical state identity remains correct;</li><li>cached and uncached expansion paths produce identical financial children;</li><li>the selected web strategy never violates the Greedy financial floor;</li><li>no Goal-mode behaviour changes;</li><li>production benchmark evidence demonstrates either a measurable runtime reduction or a clear reduction in repeated expansion work with no material memory regression;</li><li>the implementation remains bounded, deterministic and understandable.</li></ul><h2 id="production-scenario-budget-verification-24-month-sensitivity" tabindex="-1">Production-scenario budget verification (24-month sensitivity) <a class="header-anchor" href="#production-scenario-budget-verification-24-month-sensitivity" aria-label="Permalink to “Production-scenario budget verification (24-month sensitivity)”">​</a></h2><p>Executed the repository&#39;s <code>scripts/gaml-sensitivity.js</code> on the PR branch using the existing production sensitivity scenario: LKR 300,000 across DFCC, Commercial Bank and NDB, 24-month horizon, AIT enabled, no inflows, beam width 96 and local branch limit 48.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Decision cap</th><th style="text-align:right;">Expanded states</th><th style="text-align:right;">Net interest (LKR)</th><th style="text-align:right;">Final value (LKR)</th><th style="text-align:right;">Runtime</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">500</td><td style="text-align:right;">500</td><td style="text-align:right;">63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">363,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">803.89 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">1,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">363,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,590.02 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">2,500</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,500</td><td style="text-align:right;">63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">363,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">3,100.42 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">5,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,791</td><td style="text-align:right;">63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">363,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">3,232.45 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">10,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,791</td><td style="text-align:right;">63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">363,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">3,203.83 ms</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 5,000 and 10,000 caps both naturally exhausted the search after 2,791 expansions; the configured cap was not reached. All rows were financially valid and finalized. <code>optimality_proven</code> remained false, so this is evidence of budget-quality preservation for this scenario, not proof of global optimality or a universal guarantee across every input.</p><p>Before the fix, the same 24-month scenario returned LKR 63,302.37 at 500 decisions but LKR 61,886.03 at both 1,000 and 5,000. The updated implementation now retains the 500-decision incumbent through later checkpoints, and all tested decision caps return LKR 63,302.37.</p><p>Benchmark workflow: <a href="https://github.com/aaron-fredrick/FD-Calculator/actions/runs/42409787756" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://github.com/aaron-fredrick/FD-Calculator/actions/runs/42409787756</a> Artifact: absolute-r-plus-sensitivity (includes the full JSON dataset and raw point output).</p><h2 id="follow-on-hot-path-optimisation-pass" tabindex="-1">Follow-on hot-path optimisation pass <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-on-hot-path-optimisation-pass" aria-label="Permalink to “Follow-on hot-path optimisation pass”">​</a></h2><p>A code-level review identified repeated work in bounded local-combination generation and repeated immutable balance-array rewrites.</p><h3 id="local-combination-ranking" tabindex="-1">Local-combination ranking <a class="header-anchor" href="#local-combination-ranking" aria-label="Permalink to “Local-combination ranking”">​</a></h3><p>Previously, the bounded branch builder recalculated option interest/goal scores from inside the option sort comparator. Sorting invokes its comparator O(O log O) times, so score lookup and cache-key work were repeated even though each option&#39;s score is invariant for the current account, date and search state. It also recalculated the equivalent-eFD count by traversing every partial choice inside each partial-sort comparison.</p><p>The bounded path now:</p><ul><li>calculates each eligible option&#39;s score once per account/state;</li><li>calculates its equivalent-eFD tie-break metadata once;</li><li>sorts the precomputed records while preserving the existing 1e-9 score tie tolerance;</li><li>carries the accumulated equivalent-eFD count on each partial candidate, avoiding repeated choice-array scans;</li><li>carries that count through null choices unchanged.</li></ul><p>This removes repeated comparator work. The ranking still sorts the same candidate set and uses the same score and tie-break semantics. The unbounded Goal enumeration path is unchanged.</p><h3 id="batched-balance-zeroing" tabindex="-1">Batched balance zeroing <a class="header-anchor" href="#batched-balance-zeroing" aria-label="Permalink to “Batched balance zeroing”">​</a></h3><p>Consolidation and local-booking expansion previously called setSearchBalance repeatedly. Each call mapped the complete account-balance array, producing O(A * K) work for K balances zeroed across A accounts. Added zeroSearchBalances, which builds a set of changed account IDs and maps the canonical balance array once: O(A + K) expected time. It preserves the existing account order and unchanged balance values. This helper is covered by a focused primitive test.</p><h3 id="deliberately-deferred-items" tabindex="-1">Deliberately deferred items <a class="header-anchor" href="#deliberately-deferred-items" aria-label="Permalink to “Deliberately deferred items”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Pending-flow merge optimisation: current representation and merge semantics include date/account aggregation and cent rounding. A sorted-merge rewrite needs differential tests for duplicate dates/accounts and rounding-order equivalence before adoption.</li><li>Incremental state hashes: the state is immutable by convention but child construction and nested flow/obligation arrays need a strict ownership contract. A fingerprint shortcut could introduce collisions or stale identities; JSON canonical identity remains authoritative.</li><li>Transfer-fee precomputation: the account-pair count is small and the fee config is stable. It should only be added if profiles show fee lookup is material.</li><li>Weighted expansion-cache eviction: the current cache is entry-bounded but not child-count-bounded. A weighted limit is a sensible follow-up if heap profiling shows material retained-child memory. No arbitrary memory cap is introduced without measurement.</li><li>Cash-flow schedule caching: amounts, dates, AIT, compounding and leap-year settings all affect results. Existing option-interest caching does not cover complete cash-flow schedules; add only with a complete key and evidence of repeated identical schedules.</li></ul><h3 id="complexity-effect" tabindex="-1">Complexity effect <a class="header-anchor" href="#complexity-effect" aria-label="Permalink to “Complexity effect”">​</a></h3><p>For the bounded local-combination builder, option score computation changes from repeated comparator-time lookups (up to O(O log O) lookups per account) to O(O) score computations plus O(O log O) numeric comparisons. Equivalent-eFD counting changes from repeated O(A) scans per comparison to O(1) comparison using a carried count; each candidate still incurs O(A) choice-array construction, which is required by the current branch representation.</p><p>For balance zeroing, K sequential full-array updates change from O(AK) to O(A+K) expected time. These are operation-count improvements; no wall-clock speedup is claimed until the updated CI benchmark completes on the current head.</p><h3 id="current-verification-state" tabindex="-1">Current verification state <a class="header-anchor" href="#current-verification-state" aria-label="Permalink to “Current verification state”">​</a></h3><p>The new primitive unit test and ranking/balance changes are on the same PR branch. Node tests, Absolute R+ sensitivity, Scoped strategy benchmarks, documentation build, CodeQL and dependency review have been triggered for the current head. Results must be recorded here after those workflows finish. Financial parity and the production sensitivity results remain the acceptance gate; runtime improvement alone is insufficient.</p><h2 id="follow-on-optimisation-benchmark-results" tabindex="-1">Follow-on optimisation benchmark results <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-on-optimisation-benchmark-results" aria-label="Permalink to “Follow-on optimisation benchmark results”">​</a></h2><p>The updated Node.js 22 full test suite passed on commit 65f417554ed480e36ecf088ee91db3823dd681c5. The dedicated Absolute R+ sensitivity workflow also completed successfully on that exact commit: <a href="https://github.com/aaron-fredrick/FD-Calculator/actions/runs/42410672952" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://github.com/aaron-fredrick/FD-Calculator/actions/runs/42410672952</a></p><p>The 24-month, beam-96/local-branch-48 decision-cap sweep returned:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Decision cap</th><th style="text-align:right;">Expanded states</th><th style="text-align:right;">Net interest</th><th style="text-align:right;">Runtime</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">500</td><td style="text-align:right;">500</td><td style="text-align:right;">LKR 63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,403.23 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">1,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">LKR 63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,669.96 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">2,500</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,500</td><td style="text-align:right;">LKR 63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">4,911.05 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">5,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,791</td><td style="text-align:right;">LKR 63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">5,033.19 ms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">10,000</td><td style="text-align:right;">2,791</td><td style="text-align:right;">LKR 63,302.37</td><td style="text-align:right;">4,930.90 ms</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 5,000/10,000 caps naturally exhausted at 2,791 states. The net-interest result and final value remained unchanged from the pre-micro-optimisation baseline for every decision-cap point. All points finalized and passed the benchmark&#39;s financial validity checks; optimality remains unproven.</p><p>The 5,000-cap runtime is 5.033 s versus 5.833 s in the immediately preceding checkpoint implementation&#39;s recorded run, approximately 13.7% lower. This is a useful initial signal, not a controlled performance guarantee: these are single CI-run timings and should be confirmed with repeated runs on the same runner class before making a strong claim. The benchmark&#39;s beam-width and local-branch sweeps also preserve the observed quality/runtime trade-off; no production defaults were changed.</p><p>Current-head verification:</p><ul><li>Node.js 22 full suite and syntax/build checks: passed.</li><li>Absolute R+ sensitivity: passed.</li><li>Documentation build/layout: passed.</li><li>CodeQL: passed.</li><li>Dependency review: passed.</li><li>Scoped strategy benchmark coordinator skipped its matrix because an equivalent fingerprinted benchmark run was already active; the new balance primitive is also covered by the full suite. A fresh full matrix is still desirable before merge.</li></ul><h2 id="repeated-same-runner-timing-protocol" tabindex="-1">Repeated same-runner timing protocol <a class="header-anchor" href="#repeated-same-runner-timing-protocol" aria-label="Permalink to “Repeated same-runner timing protocol”">​</a></h2><p>The Absolute R+ sensitivity harness now performs one unmeasured warm-up followed by seven measured executions of the exact baseline configuration (beam width 96, local branch limit 48, decision cap 5,000) within the same Node.js process. It checks that every repetition finalizes, passes financial validation, and produces identical rounded net interest and final value. The output JSON records each duration, median, nearest-rank p95, minimum and maximum. This avoids treating one CI duration as a stable performance estimate. The expanded Scoped Range matrix and this repeated timing sweep are being rerun against the current PR head; results are not accepted until both workflows complete successfully.</p>`,83)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};